/**
@page working_with_conditional_formatting Working with Conditional Formatting

@tableofcontents

Conditional formatting is a feature of Excel that allows you to apply a format
to a cell, or a range of cells, based on definable criteria. For example you
might format cells that are >= 50 in red and cells that < 50 in green. Like
this:

@image html conditional_format1.png

There are also types of conditional formats such as color scales and data bars
that highlight the relative difference of cells in a range:

@image html conditional_format2.png

`Xlsxwriter++` supports almost all of Excel's conditional formatting types and
options, which are explained in the sections below.

@section ww_cond_format_start Getting started with conditional formatting

Conditional formatting is a feature in Excel that allows you to format cells
based on criteria such as greater than or less than, top or bottom 10 items,
or items that are above the average for the range.

The best place to start with conditional formats is in Excel. Create an
example of the type for conditional format that you want and then translate
that into an `Xlsxwriter++` program using the
`worksheet_t::conditional_format_cell()` or
`worksheet_t::conditional_format_range()` functions and the
`conditional_format_t` structure. We will walk through an example of this
below.

@subsection ww_cond_format_translate Convert an Excel conditional format to `Xlsxwriter++`

Say you wanted to highlight in red all the cells in the range `"B1:B9"`
that have a value less than 33. To do this in Excel you would select the
range, click on "Conditional Formatting" and create a rule like this:

@image html conditional_format11.png

In the image above we see the "type" which is "Cell", the "Criteria" which is
"less than", the "Value" which is 33 and the "Custom Format" which is red
text. To replicate this in `Xlsxwriter++` we will start by creating the
format.

We do this in the same way we create any worksheet format to use in
`Xlsxwriter++` by creating a `format_t` object using workbook_add_format():

@code
  // Add a format with red text.
  xwpp::format_t* custom_format = workbook.add_format();
  custom_format->set_font_color(xwpp::color_t::RED);
@endcode

The formats used for conditional formats are the same as any other worksheet
format, apart from the fact that Excel restricts some of the available
properties. We will look at that below in @ref ww_cond_format_format.

Next we create a `conditional_format_t` object. We will use this to set the
properties of the conditional format.

We then set the "Type", "Criteria" and "Value" like in the Excel dialog above:

@code
  // Create a conditional format object.
  xwpp::conditional_format_t conditional_format{
    .type_     = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::CELL,
    .criteria_ = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::LESS_THAN,
    .value_    = 33,
  };
@endcode

We then add the format we created earlier which, again, this is like the Excel
dialog:

@code
  // Set the format to use if the criteria/value applies:
  conditional_format.format_ = custom_format;
@endcode

And finally we apply the conditional format to the desired range:

@code
  // Now apply the format to data range.
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("B1:B9"), conditional_format);
@endcode

After we compile and run the program the output would look like this:

@image html conditional_format12.png

The complete version of this example is shown in @ref
conditional_format1.cpp. There is also another example that shows more advanced
conditional formatting options, see @ref conditional_format2.cpp.

@subsection ww_cond_format_struct Defining a conditional format in `Xlsxwriter++`

The `conditional_format_t` struct is used to define a conditional format in
`Xlsxwriter++`. It has number of fields, most of which are optional. The main
ones are explained here, as an introduction, and in more detail in the
sections below. For reference here is our example from the previous section:

@code
  conditional_format.type_     = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::CELL;
  conditional_format.criteria_ = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::LESS_THAN;
  conditional_format.value_    = 33;
  conditional_format.format_   = custom_format;
@endcode

The `%type_` field in is a required parameter and it has no default value. This
defines which type of Excel conditional format will be used.

The `%criteria_` field defines the criteria used to evaluate the conditional
format.  Most conditional formats will have a `%criteria_`, in which case it is
a required field, but some types such as `%duplicate_` or `%blanks_` don't need to
specify one since it is implicit in the type.

The `%value_` or `%value_string_` field sets the value to which the criteria is
applied.  Most conditional formats will have a value, or range of values, to
which the `%criteria_` is applied, but some types or criteria don't need one.

Finally, the `%format_` field defines the `format_t` that will be applied if
the conditional format matches.  Most conditional formats will used the
`%format_` field, since that is somewhat the point. However, it isn't actually
required, in Excel or in `Xlsxwriter++`, and conditional format types such as
Color Scale or Data Bars don't need it since they rely on simpler color
definitions.

The allowable `%type_` values withe their descriptive and enum values are shown
in the following table along with their associated parameters.

| Type          |  Enum value                                   | Parameters                      |
| :------------ | :-------------------------------------------- | :------------------------------ |
| cell          | conditional_format_types_t::CELL              | criteria                        |
| ^             | ^                                             | value                           |
| ^             | ^                                             | value_string                    |
| ^             | ^                                             | min_value                       |
| ^             | ^                                             | max_value                       |
| ^             | ^                                             | min_value_string                |
| ^             | ^                                             | max_value_string                |
| ^             | ^                                             | format                          |
| text          | conditional_format_types_t::TEXT              | criteria                        |
| ^             | ^                                             | value                           |
| ^             | ^                                             | value_string                    |
| ^             | ^                                             | format                          |
| time_period   | conditional_format_types_t::TIME_PERIOD       | criteria                        |
| ^             | ^                                             | format                          |
| average       | conditional_format_types_t::AVERAGE           | criteria                        |
| ^             | ^                                             | format                          |
| duplicate     | conditional_format_types_t::DUPLICATE         | format                          |
| unique        | conditional_format_types_t::UNIQUE            | format                          |
| top           | conditional_format_types_t::TOP               | criteria                        |
| ^             | ^                                             | value                           |
| ^             | ^                                             | value_string                    |
| ^             | ^                                             | format                          |
| bottom        | conditional_format_types_t::BOTTOM            | criteria                        |
| ^             | ^                                             | value                           |
| ^             | ^                                             | value_string                    |
| ^             | ^                                             | format                          |
| blanks        | conditional_format_types_t::BLANKS            | format                          |
| no_blanks     | conditional_format_types_t::NO_BLANKS         | format                          |
| errors        | conditional_format_types_t::ERRORS            | format                          |
| no_errors     | conditional_format_types_t::NO_ERRORS         | format                          |
| formula       | conditional_format_types_t::FORMULA           | value                           |
| ^             | ^                                             | value_string                    |
| ^             | ^                                             | format                          |
| 2_color_scale | conditional_format_types_t::TWO_COLOR_SCALE   | min_rule_type                   |
| ^             | ^                                             | max_rule_type                   |
| ^             | ^                                             | min_value                       |
| ^             | ^                                             | max_value                       |
| ^             | ^                                             | min_value_string                |
| ^             | ^                                             | max_value_string                |
| ^             | ^                                             | min_color                       |
| ^             | ^                                             | max_color                       |
| 3_color_scale | conditional_format_types_t::THREE_COLOR_SCALE | min_rule_type                   |
| ^             | ^                                             | mid_rule_type                   |
| ^             | ^                                             | max_rule_type                   |
| ^             | ^                                             | min_value                       |
| ^             | ^                                             | mid_value                       |
| ^             | ^                                             | max_value                       |
| ^             | ^                                             | min_value_string                |
| ^             | ^                                             | mid_value_string                |
| ^             | ^                                             | max_value_string                |
| ^             | ^                                             | min_color                       |
| ^             | ^                                             | mid_color                       |
| ^             | ^                                             | max_color                       |
| data_bar      | conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR          | min_rule_type                   |
| ^             | ^                                             | max_rule_type                   |
| ^             | ^                                             | min_value                       |
| ^             | ^                                             | max_value                       |
| ^             | ^                                             | min_value_string                |
| ^             | ^                                             | max_value_string                |
| ^             | ^                                             | bar_only                        |
| ^             | ^                                             | bar_color                       |
| ^             | ^                                             | bar_solid*                      |
| ^             | ^                                             | bar_negative_color*             |
| ^             | ^                                             | bar_border_color*               |
| ^             | ^                                             | bar_negative_border_color*      |
| ^             | ^                                             | bar_negative_color_same*        |
| ^             | ^                                             | bar_negative_border_color_same* |
| ^             | ^                                             | bar_no_border*                  |
| ^             | ^                                             | bar_direction*                  |
| ^             | ^                                             | bar_axis_position*              |
| ^             | ^                                             | bar_axis_color*                 |
| ^             | ^                                             | data_bar_2010*                  |
| icon_set      | conditional_format_types_t::ICON_SETS         | icon_style                      |
| ^             | ^                                             | reverse_icons                   |
| ^             | ^                                             | icons_only                      |

@note Data bar parameters marked with `*` are only available in Excel 2010 and
later. Files that use these properties can still be opened in Excel 2007
but the data bars will be displayed without them.

Each conditional format type is explained in more detail in the sections below.

@section ww_cond_format_type Conditional Formatting Types

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_cell type: cell

This is the most common conditional formatting type. It is used when a format
is applied to a cell, or range of cells, based on a simple criterion.

For example using a single value and the "greater than" criteria:

@code
  conditional_format.type_     = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::CELL;
  conditional_format.criteria_ = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::GREATER_THAN;
  conditional_format.value_    = 5;
  conditional_format.format_   = format;

  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

Or, using 2 values and the `between` criteria:

@code
  conditional_format.type_       = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::CELL;
  conditional_format.criteria_   = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::BETWEEN;
  conditional_format.min_value_  = 20;
  conditional_format.max_value_  = 30;
  conditional_format.format_     = format;

  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

If the `criteria` is "equal to" and the `value_string` is a text string (but
not a cell range) then Excel requires that the text string is double quoted:

@code
  conditional_format.type_         = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::CELL;
  conditional_format.criteria_     = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::EQUAL_TO;
  conditional_format.value_string_ = "\"string to match\"";
@endcode

The criteria that apply to Cell conditional formats are:

- `conditional_criteria_t::EQUAL_TO`: Format cells equal to a value.

- `conditional_criteria_t::NOT_EQUAL_TO`: Format cells not equal to a value.

- `conditional_criteria_t::GREATER_THAN`: Format cells greater than a value.

- `conditional_criteria_t::LESS_THAN`: Format cells less than a value.

- `conditional_criteria_t::GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO`: Format cells greater
  than or equal to a value.

- `conditional_criteria_t::LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO`: Format cells less than or
  equal to a value.

- `conditional_criteria_t::BETWEEN`: Format cells between two values.

- `conditional_criteria_t::NOT_BETWEEN`: Format cells that is not between two
  values.

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_time_period type: time_period

The `time_period` type is used to specify Excel's "Dates Occurring" style
conditional format:

@code
  conditional_format.type_     = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::PERIOD;
  conditional_format.criteria_ = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::TIME_PERIOD_YESTERDAY;
  conditional_format.format_   = format1;
  worksheet_conditional_format_range(worksheet, RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);

  conditional_format.type_     = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::PERIOD;
  conditional_format.criteria_ = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::TIME_PERIOD_LAST_7_DAYS;
  conditional_format.format_   = format2;
  worksheet_conditional_format_range(worksheet, RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

The period is set in the `criteria` and can have one of the following values:

- `conditional_criteria_t::TIME_PERIOD_YESTERDAY`: Format cells with a date of
  yesterday.

- `conditional_criteria_t::TIME_PERIOD_TODAY`: Format cells with a date of
  today.

- `conditional_criteria_t::TIME_PERIOD_TOMORROW`: Format cells with a date of
  tomorrow.

- `conditional_criteria_t::TIME_PERIOD_LAST_7_DAYS`: Format cells with a date
  in the last 7 days.

- `conditional_criteria_t::TIME_PERIOD_LAST_WEEK`: Format cells with a date in
  the last week.

- `conditional_criteria_t::TIME_PERIOD_THIS_WEEK`: Format cells with a date in
  the current week.

- `conditional_criteria_t::TIME_PERIOD_NEXT_WEEK`: Format cells with a date in
  the next week.

- `conditional_criteria_t::TIME_PERIOD_LAST_MONTH`: Format cells with a date
  in the last month.

- `conditional_criteria_t::TIME_PERIOD_THIS_MONTH`: Format cells with a date
  in the current month.

- `conditional_criteria_t::TIME_PERIOD_NEXT_MONTH`: Format cells with a date
  in the next month.

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_text type: text

The `text` type is used to specify Excel's "Specific Text" style conditional
format. It is used to do simple string matching using the `%criteria_` and
`%value_` parameters:

@code
  conditional_format.type_         = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::TEXT;
  conditional_format.criteria_     = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::TEXT_CONTAINING;
  conditional_format.value_string_ = "foo";
  conditional_format.format_       = format1;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);

  conditional_format.type_         = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::TEXT;
  conditional_format.criteria_     = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::TEXT_NOT_CONTAINING;
  conditional_format.value_string_ = "bar";
  conditional_format.format_       = format2;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);

  conditional_format.type_         = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::TEXT;
  conditional_format.criteria_     = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::TEXT_BEGINS_WITH;
  conditional_format.value_string_ = "a";
  conditional_format.format_       = format2;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);

  conditional_format.type_         = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::TEXT;
  conditional_format.criteria_     = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::TEXT_ENDS_WITH;
  conditional_format.value_string_ = "t";
  conditional_format.format_       = format4;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

The `%value_string_` parameter should be a string with one or more characters.

The `%criteria_` for Text conditional formats can have one of the following values:

- `conditional_criteria_t::TEXT_CONTAINING`: Format cells that contain the
  specified text.

- `conditional_criteria_t::TEXT_NOT_CONTAINING`: Format cells that don't
  contain the specified text.

- `conditional_criteria_t::TEXT_BEGINS_WITH`: Format cells that begin with the
  specified text.

- `conditional_criteria_t::TEXT_ENDS_WITH`: Format cells that end with the
  specified text.

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_average type: average

The `average` type is used to specify Excel's "Average" style conditional
format:

@code
  conditional_format.type_     = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::AVERAGE;
  conditional_format.criteria_ = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::AVERAGE_ABOVE;
  conditional_format.format_   = format1;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);

  conditional_format.type_     = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::AVERAGE;
  conditional_format.criteria_ = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::AVERAGE_1_STD_DEV_ABOVE;
  conditional_format.format_   = format2;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("B1:B4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

The type of average for the conditional format range is specified by the
`%criteria_`:

- `conditional_criteria_t::AVERAGE_ABOVE`: Format cells above the average for
  the range.

- `conditional_criteria_t::AVERAGE_BELOW`: Format cells below the average for
  the range.

- `conditional_criteria_t::AVERAGE_ABOVE_OR_EQUAL`: Format cells above or
  equal to the average for the range.

- `conditional_criteria_t::AVERAGE_BELOW_OR_EQUAL`: Format cells below or
  equal to the average for the range.

- `conditional_criteria_t::AVERAGE_1_STD_DEV_ABOVE`: Format cells 1 standard
  deviation above the average for the range.

- `conditional_criteria_t::AVERAGE_1_STD_DEV_BELOW`: Format cells 1 standard
  deviation below the average for the range.

- `conditional_criteria_t::AVERAGE_2_STD_DEV_ABOVE`: Format cells 2 standard
  deviation above the average for the range.

- `conditional_criteria_t::AVERAGE_2_STD_DEV_BELOW`: Format cells 2 standard
  deviation below the average for the range.

- `conditional_criteria_t::AVERAGE_3_STD_DEV_ABOVE`: Format cells 3 standard
  deviation above the average for the range.

- `conditional_criteria_t::AVERAGE_3_STD_DEV_BELOW`: Format cells
  3 standard deviation below the average for the range.

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_duplicate type: duplicate

The `duplicate` type is used to highlight duplicate cells in a range. It
doesn't take a criteria:

@code
  conditional_format.type_   = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DUPLICATE;
  conditional_format.format_ = format1;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_unique type: unique

The `unique` type is used to highlight unique cells in a range. It doesn't
take a criteria:

@code
  conditional_format.type_   = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::UNIQUE;
  conditional_format.format_ = format1;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_top type: top

The `top` type is used to specify the top `n` values by number or
percentage in a range:

@code
  conditional_format.type_   = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::TOP;
  conditional_format.value_  = 10;
  conditional_format.format_ = format1;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

The `criteria` can be used to indicate that a percentage condition is
required:

@code
  conditional_format.type_     = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::TOP;
  conditional_format.criteria_ = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::TOP_OR_BOTTOM_PERCENT;
  conditional_format.value_    = 10;
  conditional_format.format_   = format1;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_bottom type: bottom

The `bottom` type is used to specify the bottom `n` values by number or
percentage in a range.

It takes the same parameters as `top`, see above.

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_blanks type: blanks

The `blanks` type is used to highlight blank cells in a range:

@code
  conditional_format.type_   = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::BLANKS;
  conditional_format.format_ = format1;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_no_blanks type: no_blanks

The `no_blanks` type is used to highlight non blank cells in a range. It doesn't take a criteria:

@code
  conditional_format.type_   = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::NO_BLANKS;
  conditional_format.format_ = format1;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_errors type: errors

The `errors` type is used to highlight error cells in a range. It doesn't take a criteria:

@code
  conditional_format.type_   = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::ERRORS;
  conditional_format.format_ = format1;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_no_errors type: no_errors

The `no_errors` type is used to highlight non error cells in a range. It doesn't take a criteria:

@code
  conditional_format.type_   = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::NO_ERRORS;
  conditional_format.format_ = format1;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_formula type: formula

The `formula` type is used to specify a conditional format based on a user
defined formula:

@code
  conditional_format.type_         = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::FORMULA;
  conditional_format.value_string_ = "=$A$1>5";
  conditional_format.format_       = format1;
  worksheet_conditional_format_range(worksheet, RANGE("A1:A4"), conditional_format);

  conditional_format.type_         = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::FORMULA;
  conditional_format.value_string_ = "=$A$2<80";
  conditional_format.format_       = format2;
  worksheet_conditional_format_range(worksheet, RANGE("B1:B4"), conditional_format);

  conditional_format.type_         = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::FORMULA;
  conditional_format.value_string_ = "=$A$3>$A$4";
  conditional_format.format_       = format3;
  worksheet_conditional_format_range(worksheet, RANGE("C1:C4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

Formulas must be written with the US style separator/range operator which is a
comma (not semi-colon) and should follow the same rules as
`worksheet_t::write_formula()`. See @ref ww_formulas_non_us for a full
explanation:

@code
  // This formula will cause an Excel error on load due to non-English
  // language and use of semi-colons.
  conditional_format.type_         = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::FORMULA;
  conditional_format.value_string_ = "=ODER($B2<$C2;UND($B2="";$C2>HEUTE()))";

  // This is the correct syntax.
  conditional_format.type_         = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::FORMULA;
  conditional_format.value_string_ = "=OR($B2<$C2,AND($B2="",$C2>TODAY()))";
@endcode

@note Conditional formatting formulas require some knowledge of how cell
references work in Excel. For example the formulas `"$A$1=5"` and `"A1=5"`
will give different results when applied to a range. It is best to verify the
formula in Excel before transferring it to `Xlsxwriter++`.

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_2_color_scale type: 2_color_scale

The `2_color_scale` type is used to specify Excel's "2 Color Scale" style
conditional format:

@code
  conditional_format.type_   = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::TWO_COLOR_SCALE;
  conditional_format.format_ = format1;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A12"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@image html conditional_format4.png

This conditional type can be modified with additional parameters:

@code
  conditional_format.type_          = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::TWO_COLOR_SCALE;
  conditional_format.min_rule_type_ = xwpp::conditional_format_rule_types_t::NUMBER;
  conditional_format.max_rule_type_ = xwpp::conditional_format_rule_types_t::NUMBER;
  conditional_format.min_value_     = 20;
  conditional_format.max_value_     = 80;
  conditional_format.min_color_     = xwpp::color_t(0xFF7128);
  conditional_format.max_color_     = xwpp::color_t(0xFFEF9C);
  conditional_format.format_        = format1;
@endcode

See the following sections below for more information:

- @ref ww_cond_format_min_value
- @ref ww_cond_format_max_value
- @ref ww_cond_format_min_rule_type
- @ref ww_cond_format_max_rule_type
- @ref ww_cond_format_min_color
- @ref ww_cond_format_max_color

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_3_color_scale type: 3_color_scale

The `3_color_scale` type is used to specify Excel's "3 Color Scale" style
conditional format:

@code
  conditional_format.type_   = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::THREE_COLOR_SCALE;
  conditional_format.format_ = format1;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A12"), conditional_format);
@endcode

This conditional type can be modified with additional parameters:

@code
  conditional_format.type_          = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::THREE_COLOR_SCALE;
  conditional_format.min_value_     = 10;
  conditional_format.mid_value_     = 52;
  conditional_format.max_value_     = 99;
  conditional_format.min_rule_type_ = xwpp::conditional_format_rule_types_t::NUMBER;
  conditional_format.mid_rule_type_ = xwpp::conditional_format_rule_types_t::PERCENT;
  conditional_format.max_rule_type_ = xwpp::conditional_format_rule_types_t::PERCENTILE;
  conditional_format.min_color_     = xwpp::color_t(0xC5D9F1);
  conditional_format.mid_color_     = xwpp::color_t(0x8DB4E3);
  conditional_format.max_color_     = xwpp::color_t(0x538ED5);
  conditional_format.format_        = format1;
@endcode

See the following sections below for more information:

- @ref ww_cond_format_min_value
- @ref ww_cond_format_mid_value
- @ref ww_cond_format_max_value
- @ref ww_cond_format_min_rule_type
- @ref ww_cond_format_mid_rule_type
- @ref ww_cond_format_max_rule_type
- @ref ww_cond_format_min_color
- @ref ww_cond_format_mid_color
- @ref ww_cond_format_max_color

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_data_bar type: data_bar

The `data_bar` type is used to specify Excel's "Data Bar" style conditional
format:

@code
  conditional_format.type_   = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR;
  conditional_format.format_ = format1;
  worksheet.conditional_format_rangeRANGE("A1:A12"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@image html conditional_format7.png

This conditional type can be modified with the following parameters, which are
explained in the sections below. These properties were available in the
original xlsx file specification used in Excel 2007:

- @ref ww_cond_format_min_value
- @ref ww_cond_format_max_value
- @ref ww_cond_format_min_rule_type
- @ref ww_cond_format_max_rule_type
- @ref ww_cond_format_bar_color
- @ref ww_cond_format_bar_only

Additional data bar properties were added in Excel 2010 such as solid
(non-gradient) bars and control over how negative values are displayed. These
properties can be set using the following parameters:

- @ref ww_cond_format_bar_solid
- @ref ww_cond_format_bar_negative_color
- @ref ww_cond_format_bar_border_color
- @ref ww_cond_format_bar_negative_border_color
- @ref ww_cond_format_bar_negative_color_same
- @ref ww_cond_format_bar_negative_border_color_same
- @ref ww_cond_format_bar_no_border
- @ref ww_cond_format_bar_direction
- @ref ww_cond_format_bar_axis_position
- @ref ww_cond_format_bar_axis_color
- @ref ww_cond_format_data_bar_2010

For example:

@image html conditional_format6.png

Files that use these Excel 2010 properties can still be opened in Excel 2007
but the data bars will be displayed without them.

@subsection ww_cond_format_type_icon_set type: icon_set

The `icon_set` type is used to specify a conditional format with a set of
icons such as traffic lights or arrows:

@code
  conditional_format.type_       = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::ICON_SETS;
  conditional_format.icon_style_ = xwpp::conditional_icon_types_t::THREE_ARROWS_COLORED;
  conditional_format.format_     = format1;
  worksheet.conditional_format_cell(CELL("A1"), conditional_format);
@endcode

The icon set style is specified by the `%icon_style_` parameter. Valid options are:

- `conditional_icon_types_t::THREE_ARROWS_COLORED`: Icon style: 3 colored arrows showing up, sideways and down.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::THREE_ARROWS_GRAY`: Icon style: 3 gray arrows showing up, sideways and down.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::THREE_FLAGS`: Icon style: 3 colored flags in red, yellow and green.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::THREE_TRAFFIC_LIGHTS_UNRIMMED`: Icon style: 3 traffic lights - rounded.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::THREE_TRAFFIC_LIGHTS_RIMMED`: Icon style: 3 traffic lights with a rim - squarish.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::THREE_SIGNS`: Icon style: 3 colored shapes - a circle, triangle and diamond.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::THREE_SYMBOLS_CIRCLED`: Icon style: 3 circled symbols with tick mark, exclamation and cross.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::THREE_SYMBOLS_UNCIRCLED`: Icon style: 3 symbols with tick mark, exclamation and cross.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::FOUR_ARROWS_COLORED`: Icon style: 3 colored arrows showing up, diagonal up, diagonal down and down.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::FOUR_ARROWS_GRAY`: Icon style: 3 gray arrows showing up, diagonal up, diagonal down and down.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::FOUR_RED_TO_BLACK`: Icon style: 4 circles in 4 colors going from red to black.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::FOUR_RATINGS`: Icon style: 4 histogram ratings.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::FOUR_TRAFFIC_LIGHTS`: Icon style: 4 traffic lights.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::FIVE_ARROWS_COLORED`: Icon style: 5 colored arrows showing up, diagonal up, sideways, diagonal down and down.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::FIVE_ARROWS_GRAY`: Icon style: 5 gray arrows showing up, diagonal up, sideways, diagonal down and down.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::FIVE_RATINGS`: Icon style: 5 histogram ratings.

- `conditional_icon_types_t::FIVE_QUARTERS`: Icon style: 5 quarters, from 0 to 4 quadrants filled.

@image html conditional_format5.png

The order of Icon Sets icons can be reversed by setting `%reverse_icons_` to true.

@code
  conditional_format.type_          = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::ICON_SETS;
  conditional_format.icon_style_    = xwpp::conditional_icon_types_t::THREE_TRAFFIC_LIGHTS_UNRIMMED;
  conditional_format.reverse_icons_ = true;

  worksheet.conditional_format_range(worksheet, RANGE("B4:D4"), conditional_format);
@endcode

The icons can be displayed without the cell value by settings the `%icons_only_`
parameter to true:

@code
  conditional_format.type_       = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::SETS;
  conditional_format.icon_style_ = xwpp::conditional_icon_types_t::THREE_TRAFFIC_LIGHTS_UNRIMMED;
  conditional_format.icons_only_ = true;

  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("B5:D5"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@section ww_cond_format_other_properties Conditional Format Parameters

The fields/options in the the `%conditional_format_` are used to define a
worksheet conditional format. The `%type_` field was explained in the previous
section. The sections below explain the other options.

@subsection ww_cond_format_criteria parameter: criteria

The `%criteria_` parameter is used to set the criteria by which the cell data
will be evaluated. It is defined in `%conditional_criteria_` and has no
default value. Some conditional format types such as `%duplicate_` or `%blanks_`
don't need to specify as criteria since it is implicit in the type.

The values for different conditional format types are shown in
`conditional_criteria_t` and in the `%type_` sections to which they apply,
above.

@subsection ww_cond_format_value parameter: value and value_string

The `%value_` parameter is generally used along with `%criteria_` to set the rule
by which the cell data will be evaluated:

@code
  conditional_format.criteria_ = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::EQUAL_TO;
  conditional_format.value_    = 10;
@endcode

The `value_string` parameter is used for non-numeric values. In conditional
formats this will generally be a cell reference like the following:

@code
  conditional_format.criteria_     = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::EQUAL_TO;
  conditional_format.value_string_ = "$B$1";

  // Or like this.
  conditional_format.value_string_ = "=$B$1";
@endcode

In general any `%value_string_` property that refers to a cell reference should
use an absolute cell reference, like `$B$1`, especially if the conditional
formatting is applied to a range of values. Without an absolute cell reference
the conditional format will not be applied correctly by Excel, apart from the
first cell in the formatted range. Double check in Excel if you don't get the
result that you expect.

If the `%type_` is Cell, the `%criteria_` is "equal to" and the `%value_string_` is
a text string (but not a cell range) then Excel requires that the text string
is double quoted:

@code
  conditional_format.criteria_     = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::EQUAL_TO;
  conditional_format.value_string_ = "\"string to match\"";
@endcode

@subsection ww_cond_format_format parameter: format

The `%format_` parameter is used to specify the format that will be applied to
the cell when the conditional formatting criterion is met. The format is
created using the `workbook_t::add_format()` function in the same way as cell
formats:

@code
  xwpp::format_t* format = workbook_t::add_format();
  format_set_bold(format);
  format_set_italic(format);

  conditional_format.type_     = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::CELL;
  conditional_format.criteria_ = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::GREATER_THAN;
  conditional_format.value_    = 20;
  conditional_format.format_   = format;
@endcode

Most conditional formats will have a `format_t` `%format_`, since that is
somewhat the point. However, it isn't actually required, in Excel or in
`Xlsxwriter++`. Color Scale and Data Bar conditional format types use defined
colors instead of a format.

@note In Excel, a conditional format is superimposed over the existing cell format
and <b>not all cell format properties can be modified in a conditional
format</b>. Properties that cannot be modified in a conditional format in
Excel are: font name, font size, superscript and subscript, diagonal borders,
all alignment properties and all protection properties.

@note Excel specifies some default formats when using the conditional formatting
dialog such as red, yellow and green. These can be replicated using the
following `Xlsxwriter++` formats:

@code
  // Light red fill with dark red text.
  xwpp::format_t* format1 = workbook.add_format();
  format1->set_bg_color(xwpp:color_t(0xFFC7CE));
  format1->set_font_color(xwpp:color_t(0x9C0006));

  // Light yellow fill with dark yellow text.
  xwpp::format_t* format2 = workbook.add_format();
  format2->set_bg_color(xwpp::color_t(0xFFEB9C));
  format2->set_font_color(xwpp::color_t(0x9C6500));

  // Green fill with dark green text.
  xwpp::format_t* format3 = workbook.add_format();
  format3->set_bg_color(xwpp::color_t(0xC6EFCE));
  format3->set_font_color(xwpp::color_t(0x006100));
@endcode

See also `format_t` and @ref working_with_formats.

@subsection ww_cond_format_min_value parameter: min_value and min_value_string

The `%min_value_` parameter is used to set the lower limiting value for Cell,
Color Scale and Data Bar conditional formats.

For cell formats it is usually used when the `%criteria_` is either
`xwpp::xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::BETWEEN` or `xwpp::xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::NOT_BETWEEN`:

@code
  conditional_format.type_      = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::CELL;
  conditional_format.criteria_  = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::BETWEEN;
  conditional_format.min_value_ = 2;
  conditional_format.max_value_ = 6;
@endcode

The `min_value_string` parameter is used for non-numeric values. In conditional
formats this will generally be a cell reference like `$B$1`:

@code
  conditional_format.type_             = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::CELL;
  conditional_format.criteria_         = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::BETWEEN;
  conditional_format.min_value_string_ = "$B$1;
  conditional_format.max_value_string_ = "$B$2";
@endcode

@subsection ww_cond_format_mid_value parameter: mid_value and mid_value_string

The `%mid_value_` parameter is used to set the middle limit for Color Scale and
Data Bar conditional formats.

See @ref ww_cond_format_min_value above.

@subsection ww_cond_format_max_value parameter: max_value and max_value_string

The `%max_value_` parameter is used to set the lower limiting value for Cell,
Color Scale and Data Bar conditional formats.

See @ref ww_cond_format_min_value above.

@subsection ww_cond_format_min_rule_type parameter: min_rule_type

The `%min_rule_type_` and `%max_rule_type_` properties are used when the
conditional formatting type is `2_color_scale`, `3_color_scale` or
`data_bar`. The `%mid_rule_type_` is used with `3_color_scale`. The properties
are used as follows:

@code
  conditional_format.type_          = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::TWO_COLOR_SCALE;
  conditional_format.min_rule_type_ = xwpp::conditional_format_rule_types_t::PERCENT;
  conditional_format.max_rule_type_ = xwpp::conditional_format_rule_types_t::PERCENT;
  conditional_format.min_value_     = 20;
  conditional_format.max_value_     = 80;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A12"), conditional_format);
@endcode

The available min/mid/max rule types are defined in
`conditional_format_rule_types_t`:

- `conditional_format_rule_types_t::MINIMUM`: Conditional format rule type: matches the minimum values in the range. Can only be applied to min_rule_type.

- `conditional_format_rule_types_t::NUMBER`: Conditional format rule type: use a number to set the bound.

- `conditional_format_rule_types_t::PERCENT`: Conditional format rule type: use a percentage to set the bound.

- `conditional_format_rule_types_t::PERCENTILE`: Conditional format rule type: use a percentile to set the bound.

- `conditional_format_rule_types_t::FORMULA`: Conditional format rule type: use a formula to set the bound.

- `conditional_format_rule_types_t::MAXIMUM`: Conditional format rule type: matches the maximum values in the range. Can only be applied to max_rule_type.

@subsection ww_cond_format_mid_rule_type parameter: mid_rule_type

Used for `3_color_scale`. Same as `%min_rule_type_`, see above.

@subsection ww_cond_format_max_rule_type parameter: max_rule_type

Same as `%min_rule_type_`, see above.

@subsection ww_cond_format_min_color parameter: min_color

The `%min_color_` and `%max_color_` properties are available when the conditional
formatting type is `2_color_scale` or `3_color_scale`. The `%mid_color_` is
available for `3_color_scale`. The properties are used as follows:

@code
  conditional_format.type_      = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::TWO_COLOR_SCALE;
  conditional_format.min_color_ = xwpp::color_t(0xFF7128);
  conditional_format.max_color_ = xwpp::color_t(0xFFEF9C);
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A12"), conditional_format);
@endcode

The color can be a Html style `RRGGBB` hex number or a limited number of named
colors, see @ref working_with_colors.

@subsection ww_cond_format_mid_color parameter: mid_color

Used for `3_color_scale`. Same as `%min_color_`, see above.

@subsection ww_cond_format_max_color parameter: max_color

Same as `%min_color_`, see above.

@subsection ww_cond_format_bar_color parameter: bar_color

The `%bar_color_` parameter sets the fill color for data bars:

@code
  conditional_format.type_      = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR;
  conditional_format.bar_color_ = xwpp::color_t(0x63C384);
@endcode

@image html conditional_format7.png

The color can be a Html style `RRGGBB` hex number or a limited number of named
colors, see @ref working_with_colors.

@subsection ww_cond_format_bar_only parameter: bar_only

The `%bar_only_` property displays a bar data but not the data in the cells:

@code
  conditional_format.type_     = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR;
  conditional_format.bar_only_ = true;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A12"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@see The image above.

@subsection ww_cond_format_bar_solid parameter: bar_solid

The `%bar_solid_` property turns on a solid (non-gradient) fill for data
bars:

@code
  conditional_format.type_      = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR;
  conditional_format.bar_solid_ = true;
  worksheet-conditional_format_range(RANGE("A1:A12"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@see The image above.

@note This property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later.

@subsection ww_cond_format_bar_negative_color parameter: bar_negative_color

The `%bar_negative_color_` property sets the color fill for the negative
portion of a data bar:

@code
  conditional_format.type_               = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR;
  conditional_format.bar_negative_color_ = xwpp::color_t(0xFFFF00);
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A3:C3"), conditional_format);
@endcode

The color can be a Html style `RRGGBB` hex number or a limited number of named
colors, see @ref working_with_colors.

@note This property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later.

@subsection ww_cond_format_bar_border_color parameter: bar_border_color

The `%bar_border_color_` property sets the color for the border line of a data
bar:

@code
  conditional_format.type_             = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR;
  conditional_format.bar_color_        = xwpp::color_t(0xFF555A);
  conditional_format.bar_border_color_ = xwpp::color_t(0xFF0000);
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A3:C3"), conditional_format);
@endcode

The color can be a Html style `RRGGBB` hex number or a limited number of named
colors, see @ref working_with_colors.

@note This property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later.

@subsection ww_cond_format_bar_negative_border_color parameter: bar_negative_border_color

The `%bar_negative_border_color_` property sets the color for the border of
the negative portion of a data bar:

@code
  conditional_format.type_                      = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR;
  conditional_format.bar_negative_border_color_ = xwpp::color_t(0x92D050);
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A2:B2"), conditional_format);
@endcode

The color can be a Html style `RRGGBB` hex number or a limited number of named
colors, see @ref working_with_colors.

@note This property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later.

@subsection ww_cond_format_bar_negative_color_same parameter: bar_negative_color_same

The `%bar_negative_color_same_` property sets the fill color for the negative
portion of a data bar to be the same as the fill color for the positive
portion of the data bar:

@code
  conditional_format.type_                    = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR;
  conditional_format.bar_negative_color_same_ = true;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A3:C3"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@image html conditional_format6.png

@note This property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later.

@subsection ww_cond_format_bar_negative_border_color_same parameter: bar_negative_border_color_same

The `%bar_negative_border_color_same_` property sets the border color for the
negative portion of a data bar to be the same as the border color for the
positive portion of the data bar:

@code
  conditional_format.type_                           = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR;
  conditional_format.bar_negative_border_color_same_ = true;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A3:C3"), conditional_format);
@endcode

See The image above.

Note, this property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later.

@subsection ww_cond_format_bar_no_border parameter: bar_no_border

The `%bar_no_border_` property turns off the border for data bars:

@code
  conditional_format.type_          = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR;
  conditional_format.bar_no_border_ = true;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A2:B2"), conditional_format);
@endcode

Note, this property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later, however the
default in Excel 2007 is to not have a border.

@subsection ww_cond_format_bar_direction parameter: bar_direction

The `%bar_direction_` property sets the direction for data bars. This property
can be either one of the values from `conditional_format_bar_direction_t`
shown below. If the property isn't set then Excel will adjust the position
automatically based on the context. The available options are:

- `conditional_format_bar_direction_t::CONTEXT`: Data bar direction is set by Excel
  based on the context of the data displayed.

- `conditional_format_bar_direction_t::RIGHT_TO_LEFT`: Data bar direction is from
  right to left.

- `conditional_format_bar_direction_t::LEFT_TO_RIGHT`: Data bar direction is from
  left to right.

Here is an example:

@code
  conditional_format.type_          = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR;
  conditional_format.bar_direction_ = xwpp::conditional_format_bar_direction_t::RIGHT_TO_LEFT;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A2:B2"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@image html conditional_format6.png

@note This property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later.

@subsection ww_cond_format_bar_axis_position parameter: bar_axis_position

The `%bar_axis_position_` property sets the position of the axis that is shown
in data bars when there are negative values to display. The property can be
either `midpoint` or `none`.

@code
  conditional_format.type_              = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR;
  conditional_format.bar_axis_position_ = xwpp::conditional_bar_axis_position_t::MIDPOINT;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A2:B12"), conditional_format);
@endcode

If the property isn't set then Excel will position the axis based on the range
of positive and negative values. The setting are defined in
`conditional_bar_axis_position_t`:

- `conditional_bar_axis_position_t::AUTOMATIC`: Data bar axis position is set by Excel based on the context of the data displayed.

- `conditional_bar_axis_position_t::MIDPOINT`: Data bar axis position is set at the midpoint.

- `conditional_bar_axis_position_t::NONE`: Data bar axis is turned off.

@note This property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later.

@subsection ww_cond_format_bar_axis_color parameter: bar_axis_color

The `%bar_axis_color_` property sets the color for the axis that is shown in
data bars when there are negative values to display:

@code
  conditional_format.type_           = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR;
  conditional_format.bar_axis_color_ = xwpp::color_t(0x0070C0);
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A3:C13"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@note This property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later.

@subsection ww_cond_format_data_bar_2010 parameter: data_bar_2010

The `%data_bar_2010_` property sets Excel 2010 style data bars even when Excel
2010 specific properties aren't used. This can be used for ensure conformity
across all the data bar formatting in a worksheet:

@code
  conditional_format.type_          = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::DATA_BAR;
  conditional_format.data_bar_2010_ = true;
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("A3:C13"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@subsection ww_cond_format_stop_if_true parameter: stop_if_true

The `%stop_if_true_` parameter can be used to set the "stop if true" feature
of a conditional formatting rule when more than one rule is applied to a cell
or a range of cells. When this parameter is set then subsequent rules are not
evaluated if the current rule is true:

@code
  conditional_format.type_         = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::CELL;
  conditional_format.criteria_     = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::GREATER_THAN;
  conditional_format.value_        = 5;
  conditional_format.stop_if_true_ = true;
  worksheet.conditional_format_cell(CELL("A1"), conditional_format);
@endcode

@subsection ww_cond_format_multi_range parameter: multi_range

The `%multi_range_` option is used to extend a conditional format over
non-contiguous ranges.

It is possible to apply the conditional format to different cell ranges in a
worksheet using multiple calls to `worksheet_t::conditional_format()`. However,
as a minor optimization it is also possible in Excel to apply the same
conditional format to different non-contiguous cell ranges.

This is replicated in `worksheet_t::conditional_format()` using the `multi_range`
option. The range must contain the primary range for the conditional format
and any others separated by spaces.

For example to apply one conditional format to two ranges, `B3:K6` and
`B9:K12`:

@code
  conditional_format.type_        = xwpp::conditional_format_types_t::CELL;
  conditional_format.criteria_    = xwpp::conditional_criteria_t::GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO;
  conditional_format.value_       = 50;
  conditional_format.format_      = format1;
  conditional_format.multi_range_ = "B3:K6 B9:K12";
  worksheet.conditional_format_range(RANGE("B3:K12"), conditional_format);
@endcode

Next: @ref working_with_tables

*/
